There are three typologies of modularity - slot, bus and sectional. Each typology should be selected from the designer depending on the situation and goal of the product architecture.

 •  Slot - Here, each interface between parts in a slot architecture has a distinct form from other parts, thus making it impossible to exchange the multiple parts in the item. For example, an automobile consists of different parts that are joined together but that cannot be replaced one another. 

 •  Bus - Here, different parts are joined together through a common structure. All the parts have the same interface and they can be flexible and interchanged. An easy example can be the USB port, which has the same interface for different kind of computers devices.

 •  Sectional - Here, parts have the same interface, however, no all parts can join each other in the same way. A possible example is the Ikea furniture, where a wardrobe use the same joints but not all parts can be joined with each other because the joints are placed in different distances.